WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.170
- Now, see, I know you as a
very funny stand up comedian

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.020
around LA and actually she's really good

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.280
if you haven't seen her as a stand.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.300
So now you're an actress.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.070
- Is this acting, I don't even know.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:09.990
- This is stunting.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.840
- This make you an
actress or a stunt person?

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.810
- It makes me both.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:13.950
- Oh, okay.
- Yeah.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.610
- Did you two know each other beforehand

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:16.443
or how'd his work?

00:00:16.443 --> 00:00:18.400
- We did not know each other.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.260
- I was a friend of...

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.310
I was a fan of hers on Instagram.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.060
And so I just told Jeff when
we were looking for people,

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.150
we should bring in Rachel and
talk because she's very funny.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:26.983
- [Jay] Okay.

00:00:26.983 --> 00:00:29.493
- Well, one day he slid into my DMs.

00:00:30.454 --> 00:00:31.430
Do you know what DMs are?

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.263
- Yeah, I do.

00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:33.687
I'm not that old, but yes, I know it too.

00:00:33.687 --> 00:00:36.130
But it does sound like
something invasive (laughs).

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.390
- Well, I noticed one day

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.930
Knoxville was liking a bunch
of my jokes respectfully,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.610
and I was like, "Why is Johnny
Knoxville way more supportive

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.471
than my boyfriend on Instagram?"

00:00:47.471 --> 00:00:48.304
(Knoxville laughs)

00:00:48.304 --> 00:00:50.410
And then, yeah, he slid into my DMs

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.380
and asked if I wanted
to have a conversation.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.500
And it turns out I did.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.720
So we talked and then he
invited me in for a meeting

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.080
with the director, Jeff Tremaine,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.680
and the rest is history.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.580
- Now, do you know what the
stunts are gonna be before...

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.540
Do they lay it all out
for you or you just...

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.373
- [Rachel] No.

00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:11.320
- Surprise.
- No, no.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.300
You have no idea.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.119
- So that's not really acting.

00:01:14.119 --> 00:01:14.952
(both laughing)

00:01:14.952 --> 00:01:16.670
- No, it's more like improvising.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.030
- [Jay] Yeah, just more
like hoping not to get...

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.160
- I just hope I don't die.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.000
- Yeah, exactly.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.040
- Yeah.
- Exactly.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.600
How do you know that someone is ready

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.740
to be part of the "Jackass" family?

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.960
I guess you would call it.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.810
- Well, we knew most of the
people except for like Rachel

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.570
and one other guy.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:36.520
And so we brought them in, talk to them,

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.360
see if they fit in personality wise,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.040
and then we brought them
in for a two day test

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.440
in December of 2019.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.580
Not only to test them,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.490
but to test us because we are older now

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:47.990
and we didn't want to look sweaty.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.090
- Right.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.030
- But luckily within an hour of shooting,

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.920
we're like, "This is good.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.290
We're all jelling."

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:55.700
- I just liked the, "We knew everybody

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.494
except for two of them."

00:01:57.494 --> 00:01:58.600
(Knoxville laughs)

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.080
Did you have any serious injuries?

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.420
What was your worst injury?

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.840
- I would have to say my worst injury

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.180
was probably when I got bit
in the face by a scorpion.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.080
- Bit in the face by a scorpion.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.053
All right, I mean, you seem fine.

00:02:10.053 --> 00:02:10.930
I don't see

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.660
any scorpion marks.
- I'm fine now.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.700
- Where in the face did you get bit?

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.533
- It was on my lip.

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:17.237
It's cheaper than lip injections so...

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:19.110
(Knoxville laughing)
- Yeah, I would say.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:20.430
- I'm always looking for, you know...

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.750
- I understand that.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.550
Now, it's funny 'cause
I know you as a stand up

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.150
but you also come from
a very serious family.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.010
Your mom is a judge, I believe, yeah?

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.670
- My mom, she was a judge

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.250
and my dad is currently the
district attorney of Las Vegas.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.750
- [Jay] Wow.
- [Rachel] Yeah.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.570
- So how did you get away with anything?

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.321
- I didn't get anything at all actually.

00:02:39.321 --> 00:02:40.240
- As a teenager?

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.073
- No, nothing.

00:02:41.073 --> 00:02:44.910
Like there was one time I
went with a couple of friends

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:46.370
to go see a movie in seventh grade

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.900
and I made out with a
boy in the movie theater

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.930
and by 5:00 PM that day, my mom knew.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.412
And this was before the internet.

00:02:52.412 --> 00:02:54.160
(Knoxville laughs)
- [Jay] Wow, okay.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:54.993
- Yeah.

00:02:54.993 --> 00:02:56.850
- So they have a (indistinct).

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:57.683
- I don't know.

00:02:57.683 --> 00:02:59.210
She must have spies out there.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:02.890
- Okay and I assume you were
the black sheep of the family?

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:03.723
- Definitely.

00:03:03.723 --> 00:03:04.680
(Jay speaks indistinctly)
Yeah.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:05.950
- The troubled one.
- Of course.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:06.827
- Are you the youngest?

00:03:06.827 --> 00:03:07.810
- I'm the oldest.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.560
My sister is also an-
- Oh, you're the oldest?

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.049
- Yeah, my sister is an attorney as well.

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:13.930
(Knoxville laughs)
- Oh, wow.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:15.640
Well, so at least you
got a good representation

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.110
if you get in trouble.
- Of course.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:17.943
- So what did they...

00:03:17.943 --> 00:03:19.630
I mean, were you always, "Oh, my God,

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:20.463
what are we gonna do with?"

00:03:20.463 --> 00:03:23.650
'Cause with me being dyslexic
I was not a good student

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.250
and my brother was Yale Law
and all that kind of stuff,

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.430
you know, what are we gonna do with Jay?

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.880
That was sort of the topic
when I wasn't around.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:32.713
Were you that one?

00:03:32.713 --> 00:03:34.696
Were you the one, oh, my
God, what went wrong here?

00:03:34.696 --> 00:03:36.950
- No, I mean, definitely what went wrong,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.320
but people would ask me all the time

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.530
if I was gonna go into law and I tried.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.550
I tried for like one semester,
I was horrible at it.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.020
So I stopped.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.463
- What, were you breaking it?

00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:51.270
(both laughing)

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.520
- None of it made sense to me.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.799
- Now, anything surprised you
about working with Johnny?

00:03:55.799 --> 00:03:58.600
Were you sort of like, "Oh,
this is exactly where I am.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:03:59.840
We're on the same head space

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.873
or this guy is so much
further gone than I am."

00:04:02.873 --> 00:04:04.090
What was the feeling?

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.400
- Well, it was funny because
the first day we get to set,

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.670
Knoxville looked like the Knoxville

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.280
I grew up watching on television.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.080
He had dark hair,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.680
he looked pretty much the same to me.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.330
And then as we started filming more,

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:20.270
one day he shows up

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:23.483
to set looking like silver Foxville here.

00:04:24.709 --> 00:04:25.950
- Oh, Rachel.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.880
- And I was like, "I don't
recognize this person."

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.560
And I think me and the internet both agree

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.377
that he has a strong
resemblance to Jamie Lee Curtis.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:40.230
- Jamie Lee Curtis?
(Knoxville laughs)

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.120
Do we have a photo?

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:42.703
Let's take a look.
- Let's see.

00:04:44.214 --> 00:04:45.350
Okay.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:46.966
- [Rachel] I thought I was
on the set "Apollo Weave".

00:04:46.966 --> 00:04:48.173
- Yeah, not bad.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:50.338
Not bad.

00:04:50.338 --> 00:04:52.280
But here's what I don't understand.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:53.430
In the first half of the movie,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:56.640
he had black hair and then one
day he showed up with gray-

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.540
- After COVID, during the COVID break.

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:03.450
We started filming in 2020 in February,

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.090
then we got shut down in March

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:06.800
and we resumed again in September.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.500
So between that time I became...

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.380
- Yeah, but most films have what they call

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.967
a continuity person and they will say,

00:05:12.967 --> 00:05:15.000
"Oh, you have the ring
on the wrong finger."

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.800
They'll notice the smallest thing.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.090
I mean, when you came in...

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:19.923
- There was no-
- The black hair

00:05:19.923 --> 00:05:21.050
and then the silver.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.730
Then some guy, "Hey, wait a
minute, it doesn't quite match."

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.150
- You know, we don't
have a continuity person.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:27.110
- [Jay] Really?
- Yeah.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.010
- I'm stunned.

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:29.130
- Unbelievably.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.703
- Oh, all right, okay.

